        {
          "ruleId": "IMPROPER VERIFICATION OF CRYPTOGRAPHIC SIGNATURE",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "JWT Key Confusion / Algorithm Confusion Vulnerability (RS256 to HS256): Data Path Analysis:\n- Source: Route `POST /api/v1/auth/verify` receives `body.token` from untrusted client HTTP requests and passes it to `jwtService.verifyToken(body.token)`.\n- Sanitization/Validation: `verifyToken` decodes `header.alg` directly from the untrusted token without enforcing an expected algorithm whitelist or restricting tokens to asymmetric RS256 signatures.\n- Sink: At lines 63-70, if `header.alg === 'HS256'`, the service verifies the token using `crypto.createHmac('sha256', this.publicKeyPEM)`, treating the public RSA PEM key string as the HMAC secret key. An attacker who obtains or knows the public key can craft and sign arbitrary tokens with HS256, forging any claims/roles (e.g. elevating privileges to enterprise admin) that pass signature verification.\n\nConceptual Exploit Payload:\n1. Construct a header with `\"alg\": \"HS256\"`, `\"typ\": \"JWT\"`.\n2. Construct payload with arbitrary claims:\n```json\n{\n  \"sub\": \"cust_001\",\n  \"name\": \"Attacker\",\n  \"tier\": \"TIER_ENTERPRISE\",\n  \"role\": \"admin\",\n  \"exp\": 1999999999\n}\n```\n3. Sign `base64url(header) + \".\" + base64url(payload)` using HMAC-SHA256 with the server's public key string as the HMAC key.\n4. Send verification request:\n```http\nPOST /api/v1/auth/verify HTTP/1.1\nHost: localhost:8082\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\n  \"token\": \"eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9.\u003cbase64url_payload\u003e.\u003cforged_hmac_signature\u003e\"\n}\n```"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "services/identity-service/src/services/jwt_service.js"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 63,
                  "endLine": 70
                }
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CONCURRENT EXECUTION USING SHARED RESOURCE WITH IMPROPER SYNCHRONIZATION",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Concurrency Race Condition (TOCTOU) Allowing Double-Spending in Account Debit: Data Path Analysis:\n- Source: HTTP POST endpoint `/api/v1/accounts/debit` handled in `services/core-banking/internal/handler/http.go:HandleDebit` reads JSON input (`accountId`, `amount`, `description`, `referenceId`) and calls `bankingSvc.Debit()`.\n- Sanitization/Validation: At lines 97-100, the method checks `if acc.Balance \u003c amount`. However, this read-and-check operation is performed without acquiring a mutex or performing an atomic database compare-and-swap / transaction isolation lock.\n- Sink: At line 103, an artificial ledger delay `time.Sleep(15 * time.Millisecond)` occurs between reading the balance and updating it (`newBalance := acc.Balance - amount`, `s.repo.UpdateAccount(acc)`). Because Go's HTTP server handles each request in a concurrent goroutine, multiple requests sent in parallel can pass the liquidity check before any one request commits the deducted balance, causing double-spending and draining account balances beyond allowed limits.\n\nConceptual Exploit Payload:\nSend two or more concurrent HTTP requests in parallel for an account with balance 1250.00:\n```http\nPOST /api/v1/accounts/debit HTTP/1.1\nHost: localhost:8081\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\n  \"accountId\": \"acc_1002\",\n  \"amount\": 1000.0,\n  \"description\": \"Exploit Debit 1\",\n  \"referenceId\": \"ref_race_1\"\n}\n```\nand concurrently:\n```http\nPOST /api/v1/accounts/debit HTTP/1.1\nHost: localhost:8081\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\n  \"accountId\": \"acc_1002\",\n  \"amount\": 1000.0,\n  \"description\": \"Exploit Debit 2\",\n  \"referenceId\": \"ref_race_2\"\n}\n```\nBoth goroutines observe `acc.Balance == 1250.0 \u003e= 1000.0` during the 15ms window, and both proceed to debit, resulting in a total of 2000.0 debited from an account holding only 1250.0."
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "services/core-banking/internal/service/banking.go"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 96,
                  "endLine": 111
                }
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "AUTHORIZATION BYPASS THROUGH USER-CONTROLLED KEY",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Broken Object Level Authorization (BOLA/IDOR) in Account Statement Generation: Data Path Analysis:\n- Source: Route `GET /api/v1/accounts/{id}/statement` receives the target account ID from the URL path and tenant identity from the `X-Customer-ID` request header.\n- Sanitization/Validation: Although `customerID` is extracted in `HandleGetStatement` and passed into `GetStatement(customerID, accountID)`, `GetStatement` never checks `acc.CustomerID == customerID`.\n- Sink: The method queries the repository for the account (`s.repo.GetAccountByID(accountID)`) and returns all transaction entries and balances for that account. Additionally, `statementCache` uses a global key `fmt.Sprintf(\"stmt:%s\", accountID)` completely lacking customer/tenant scoping. Any caller with access to the API can inspect statements and sensitive financial records belonging to any other customer/tenant.\n\nConceptual Exploit Payload:\nAn unprivileged user (`cust_001` or `cust_002`) sends a request for corporate account `acc_1003` (belonging to `cust_003`):\n```http\nGET /api/v1/accounts/acc_1003/statement HTTP/1.1\nHost: localhost:8081\nX-Customer-ID: cust_001\n```\nThe server returns 200 OK with the full transaction ledger and account balance (1,500,000.00 BRL) belonging to `cust_003`."
          },
