          "ruleId": "Use of Hard-coded Credentials",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Hardcoded Default Cryptographic Secret Fallback for Partner HMAC Authentication: Data Path Analysis:\n- Source: Route `POST /api/v1/risk/partner-verify` receives HTTP request body and incoming HMAC signature from `X-Partner-Signature` header.\n- Sanitization/Validation: In `auth.py`, if the `PARTNER_HMAC_SECRET` environment variable is not explicitly supplied in the deployment configuration, the verifier falls back to the static string `\"cymbal_default_internal_sec_2026\"`.\n- Sink: At lines 12-14, the hardcoded fallback secret is used by `hmac.new(self.secret_key, payload, hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()` to authenticate partner API requests. Additionally, at line 24, signatures are compared with standard Python equality (`expected == incoming_signature`) instead of constant-time comparison `hmac.compare_digest`, introducing an observable timing discrepancy (CWE-208). Any attacker knowing the public/decompiled default secret can forge valid HMAC signatures to impersonate trusted partners.\n\nConceptual Exploit Payload:\nCompute the HMAC-SHA256 of any arbitrary partner verification payload using the fallback key:\n```python\nimport hmac, hashlib\npayload = b'{\"action\": \"authorize_bypass\", \"accountId\": \"acc_1001\"}'\nsecret = b'cymbal_default_internal_sec_2026'\nsig = hmac.new(secret, payload, hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()\n```\nSend request:\n```http\nPOST /api/v1/risk/partner-verify HTTP/1.1\nHost: localhost:8085\nContent-Type: application/json\nX-Partner-Signature: \u003csig\u003e\n\n{\"action\": \"authorize_bypass\", \"accountId\": \"acc_1001\"}\n```\nThe server validates the signature and returns `{\"valid\": true, \"message\": \"Signature verified\"}`."
